## C2 — parity: replay the four divergences from the saved prior state

```
PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 prototype/.venv/bin/python \
  research/quality-sprint-2026-09-30/work/M1b/probe_parity_replay.py \
  --run prototype/bench/runs/2026-09-30/v5-sprint-v2-813 \
  --out research/quality-sprint-2026-09-30/work/M1b/probe_parity_replay.json \
  --reason-source research/hop-format-audit/runs/cc3a551f-a8a5-4c0d-b90b-d3db2b26858a.excluded.json
```
**exit 0.** Stdout/stderr `tmp/probe2.stdout`, `tmp/probe2.stderr`; result
[probe_parity_replay.json](<probe_parity_replay.json>), missing-state manifest
[MISSING-STATE.json](<MISSING-STATE.json>).

| case | prior payloads recovered | prefix replayed byte-identical | prior state fully replayed | intermediate tool hops in the capture | restored tool args match saved | failing push reproduced |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| `derive-008` | 5 | **yes** (4/4 prefix, 0 hops) | **yes** | 0 | **yes** (3 minted pairs recovered: NAME, DOB, PHONE) | **no** (all 5 declared tools tried) |
| `sequential_chain:assistant-002` | 8 | yes (4/4 prefix) | no | 1 | no | no (4 tools tried) |
| `tool_chain-037` | 31 | yes (4/4 prefix) | no | 8 | no | no (8 tools tried) |
| `tool_chain-040` | 31 | yes (4/4 prefix) | no | 8 | no | no (8 tools tried) |

Byte-identity covers the payloads the replay produces — the system push and the
user pushes before the first tool call. Only `derive-008` has no intermediate hop,
so only there is the prior state replayed in full (4 replayed payloads + the
saved `tool_args` = all 5). The other three carry delivered bytes for 1/8/8
intermediate tool results that this probe does not replay: the capture holds what
was delivered but not the call that produced it.

Facts reproduced about the divergences:

- The excluded arm-I records are `_empty_run`: `state=[]`, `calls=[]`,
  `substitutes={}`, `request_count=0`; the only surviving text is the 60-character
  prefix pair inside the triple `reason`.
- The prior state **is** on disk: `GatewayV5(capture=True)` appended every payload
  as it was delivered and the triple appended the capture even after the arm
  failed (`harness.py:1343` runs after `asyncio.gather` returns an outcome). Each
  of the four captures ends at the `tool_args` payload that the failing
  `push_call` consumes — so the failing boundary is `tool_result`, and the
  pre-failure transcript, user messages and tool arguments are recovered exactly.
- In every one of the four, the production and counting prefixes **are equal to
  each other** (the divergence begins at or after character 60).
- `derive-008` reaches byte-identical prior state and the failing push still does
  not diverge under freshly minted substitutes — so the trigger is in the failing
  push's own mint/state, not in the prior transcript.
- The other three cases show the same failure to reproduce, but their prior state
  is only replayed up to the first tool call, so their «no divergence» is weaker
  evidence than `derive-008`'s and is not used to generalise.

## C3 — parity: class controls (positive / negative / echo / fragments)

```
PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 prototype/.venv/bin/python \
  research/quality-sprint-2026-09-30/work/M1b/probe_parity_mechanism.py \
  --run prototype/bench/runs/2026-09-30/v5-sprint-v2-813 --case derive-008 \
  --tool-args research/quality-sprint-2026-09-30/work/M1b/tmp/derive-008.tool_args.json \
  --out research/quality-sprint-2026-09-30/work/M1b/probe_parity_mechanism.json
```
**exit 0.** Result [probe_parity_mechanism.json](<probe_parity_mechanism.json>).

Eight controls run on `derive-008`'s exact prior state (primed vault), each asked
whether `score._mask_message(text, …, "tool")` differs from
`envelope.mask_messages(transcript+text, context, False)[-1]["content"]`:

| control | differs? |
|---|---|
| a **new** value the transcript never saw (name, birth date, phone) | no |
| **no personal data** at all (negative) | no |
| a repeat of a **substitute** the transcript already shows | no |
| lone surname fragment / phone head / phone tail / patronymic | no |
| the whole real name again | no |

`positive_differs=false`, `negative_equal=true`. The two compared functions *are*
different (production reads a frozen provenance snapshot and has a call-scoped
`_reserve_echoes` leg; `score._mask_message` has no call association, reads the
vault live, and derives its LOC cover set from the pieces it kept), but none of
these shapes makes them disagree, so the trigger stays **UNKNOWN** — recorded as
unknown, not invented.

